description:
FILED s514d (Opus, 2026-10-10) -- what the everyday row modules/Archive-Tar-Compress meets once #2590 ties its handle.

`substr(...)` passed as a SUB ARGUMENT is an LVALUE in perl: `@_` aliases it, so a write through `$_[0]` (or through `\$_[0]`) lands in the substring of the caller's string.  PCL passes the substring's VALUE, so the write is lost silently.

Reproducer (perl 5.40.3 prints `[data1]` `[head:data2]` `[xdirect]`; PCL prints `[]` `[head:]` `[xx]`):

    sub fill { my $r = \$_[0]; $$r = "data" . $_[1]; 4 }
    my $buf = ""; fill(substr($buf, 0), 1); print "[$buf]\n";
    my $b2 = "head:"; fill(substr($b2, 5), 2); print "[$b2]\n";
    sub fill2 { $_[0] = "direct" }
    my $b3 = "xx"; fill2(substr($b3, 1)); print "[$b3]\n";

Where it bites: core IO::Zlib::READ is `$self->{'file'}->gzread(substr($$bufref,$offset),$nbytes)`, and (Compress::Zlib does not load under PCL: it needs XS File::Glob) the external-gzip gzread_external reads with `read($file, $$bufref, $nbytes)` where $bufref = \$_[0] -- the substr lvalue.  So Archive::Tar->new($file) reads nothing: "Cannot read enough bytes from the tarfile".  The everyday row's baseline cause moves here from #2590/#155.

Fix direction: the argument-list lowering gives a `substr` argument an lvalue cell (the 4-arg/lvalue substr machinery already exists for `substr(...) = ...`); the same family as `p-with-buffer-place` for read's buffer.
